Reject a malformed serialized state when unserializing a compact ALTREP
integer or real sequence, so unserialize() stops reading out of bounds on
a crafted stream.

compact_intseq_Unserialize() and compact_realseq_Unserialize() in
src/main/altclasses.c reconstruct a compact sequence from its serialized
state: a length-3 numeric vector (length, first value, increment).  They
read state[0], state[1] and state[2] with no check on its type or length.
ReadItem() hands the method whatever object the stream supplied, so a
hand-crafted stream can put a shorter or wrong-typed object there: the
[1]/[2] reads then run off the end of a short vector, and a non-vector
state makes REAL0/INTEGER0 form an invalid data pointer.

    ==254==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow READ of size 4
        #0 compact_intseq_Unserialize      altclasses.c:91
        #1 altrep_UnserializeEX_default    altrep.c:694
        #2 ReadItem_Recursive              serialize.c:1925
        #3 R_Unserialize                   serialize.c:2300

oss-fuzz testcase 5857824396345344 supplies a length-1 integer state, so
reading the increment at index 2 is the overflow above; a second input
5360206129987584 null-dereferences at altclasses.c:89.  Whether the read
faults depends on heap layout (a length-1 integer state shares a vector
node with slack), so it is not caught on every build.

Validate the state (numeric vector of length >= 3) and raise a clean R
error for a malformed one, in both the integer and real constructors.
unserialize()/readRDS() of untrusted data is documented as unsafe, so
this is a robustness fix (fail cleanly rather than read out of bounds).

Found by fuzzing R with libFuzzer + AddressSanitizer (unserialize target).

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR #336; R Bugzilla report drafted, not yet
filed.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report it
as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

diff --git a/src/main/altclasses.c b/src/main/altclasses.c
index b852c5f108..58df07c327 100644
--- a/src/main/altclasses.c
+++ b/src/main/altclasses.c
@@ -86,6 +86,15 @@ static SEXP new_compact_realseq(R_xlen_t, double, double);
 
 static SEXP compact_intseq_Unserialize(SEXP class, SEXP state)
 {
+    /* state is the serialized info vector (see compact_intseq_Serialized_state):
+       a numeric vector of length 3.  A crafted serialized stream can supply
+       any object here, so reject a malformed state with a clean error rather
+       than reading REAL0/INTEGER0 off the end of a short or wrong-typed
+       (or non-vector) object. */
+    if ((TYPEOF(state) != REALSXP && TYPEOF(state) != INTSXP) ||
+	XLENGTH(state) < 3)
+	error("invalid compact_intseq serialized state");
+
     R_xlen_t n = COMPACT_INTSEQ_SERIALIZED_STATE_LENGTH(state);
     int n1 = COMPACT_INTSEQ_SERIALIZED_STATE_FIRST(state);
     int inc = COMPACT_INTSEQ_SERIALIZED_STATE_INCR(state);
@@ -363,6 +372,12 @@ static SEXP compact_realseq_Serialized_state(SEXP x)
 
 static SEXP compact_realseq_Unserialize(SEXP class, SEXP state)
 {
+    /* as for compact_intseq_Unserialize: the info vector read below is a
+       length-3 REALSXP, but a crafted stream can supply anything, so reject
+       a malformed state rather than reading REAL0 off the end. */
+    if (TYPEOF(state) != REALSXP || XLENGTH(state) < 3)
+	error("invalid compact_realseq serialized state");
+
     double inc = COMPACT_REALSEQ_INFO_INCR(state);
     R_xlen_t len = COMPACT_REALSEQ_INFO_LENGTH(state);
     double n1 = COMPACT_REALSEQ_INFO_FIRST(state);
